    <abstract id="aspace_b17a445a7b12c0c732e206e5ae053b9b">The Amote Sias papers consist of personal essays, legal and financial records, ephemera and various clippings relating to Sias's life and involvement in Brooklyn politics as well as her role as an African-American activist. The majority of the collection focuses on the political environment in Brooklyn from the mid 1970s and 1980s.</abstract>
    <langmaterial id="aspace_b8ae4fc0a55e179b2f4e1e8d62c79060">Materials principally in English, some publications are in Spanish.</langmaterial>
  </did>
  <accessrestrict id="aspace_4eaf9b73b17018a24482501c60bebd25">
    <head>Conditions Governing Access</head>
<p>Open to researchers without restriction.</p>  </accessrestrict>
  <acqinfo id="aspace_166973abd435e928228bc6c2d473151e">
    <head>Immediate Source of Acquisition</head>
<p>Gift of Amote Sias, 1998. Found in Brooklyn Historical Society's collections when the institution reopened in 2004, the materials were subsequently formally accessioned in 2008.</p>  </acqinfo>
  <arrangement id="aspace_672cab8f0c1617a6da89f9736ffce9fd">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>The Amote Sias papers are organized into three series by type: 
Series 1: Personal Papers;
Series 2: Professional Papers;
Series 3: Publications and Clippings.</p><p>Users of the collection should be aware that folders labeled with a date range might also include undated documents. Oversize materials have been placed in two oversize boxes.</p>  </arrangement>
  <bioghist id="aspace_30eeb23c30945e25a125b92ad80b4efb">
    <head>Biographical note</head>
<p>Amote Sias is a resilient African American social activist who was an active member of the Brooklyn community of Carroll Gardens and a candidate for city office during the 1970s-1980s. Sias is also a deeply committed educator and leader, having been a teacher at both the elementary and secondary level in the New York City Public School system as early as the 1970s. Sias went on to become a renowned principal at the Brooklyn Collegiate High School.</p>  </bioghist>
  <prefercite id="aspace_352838a2730834408f9ed11db73348b1">
    <head>Preferred Citation</head>
<p>Identification of item, date (if known); Amote Sias papers, 2008.017, Box and Folder number; Brooklyn Public Library, Center for Brooklyn History.</p>  </prefercite>
  <processinfo id="aspace_4355a618e0227304f7e87b897fb58fbc">
    <head>Processing Information</head>
<p>The Amote Sias papers were processed in March 2008 by Cynthia Tobar, under the supervision of Leilani Dawson. Additional processing and description were done in December 2010 by Rachel M. Oleaga. Provenance documentation was found in collection in 2026 and this finding aid was revised and updated by Dee Bowers accordingly.</p>  </processinfo>
  <relatedmaterial id="aspace_970133069ef418965c9a4f42d3872f52">
